    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nDesert Sand LS6 Is a Holy Grail 1970 Chevy Chevelle in a Very Rare Color\n14 photos\nPhoto: Patrick Glenn Nichols Musclecar Barn Finds/YouTube\nIntroduced in 1963 as Chevrolet's first full-size car, the Chevelle remained on the assembly line through 1977. Like most intermediates of the era, the Chevelle joined the muscle car wars. It also spawned the most powerful production car of the era.\nThe latter arrived for the 1970 model year with a 454-cubic-inch (7.4-liter) powerplant under the hood. It's called the LS6 and produced a whopping 450 horsepower thanks to a four-barrel Holley carburetor and heavy-duty internals. Torque was just as impressive at 500 pound-feet (678 Nm).\nThe mill also found its way into the Monte Carlo and the Corvette, but it was short-lived. It returned in 1971 in the Corvette sports car only, this time around with a detuned rating. When it comes to the Chevelle, the LS6 version is a one-year-only model.\nChevrolet sold over 400,000 Chevelles in 1970, but only 4,475 units are said to have been ordered with the mighty LS6. The number includes about 500 El Camino pickups , so we're looking at under 4,000 units with the two-door hardtop and convertible body styles (the drop-tops are super rare).\nAlthough there's no shortage of LS6 vehicles on today's classic car market, only a tiny fraction of them still have numbers-matching engines under the hood. According to Chevelle expert Patrick Glenn Nichols, only two to three out of 10 vehicles can still brag about that. We're probably talking about only a few hundred cars. This Desert Sand hardtop is one of them.\nFound in Olathe, Kansas, this Chevelle was recently removed from storage to be checked and endorsed by Mr. Nichols. And it's quite the noteworthy Chevelle. In addition to the numbers-matching drivetrain , it also has the desirable four-speed manual transmission, while the color combo is quite rare.\nSpecifically, it has a Desert Sand exterior with no vinyl top and a saddle interior. The combo was reportedly ordered on less than 3% of the LS6-fitted Chevelles, according to the official registry. That's fewer than 130 examples made, with the number of actual survivors being much smaller.\nUnlike the very similar LS6 we saw cross the block back in 2021 , this Chevelle is not Concours-ready. Although it looks pretty good inside and out, it has a few issues that need to be addressed, mostly in the mods department. Patrick found a modern power steering unit, an aftermarket fan, and incorrect front seatbelts.\nThese items didn't stop him from endorsing the car, though. After all, it's a mostly original LS6 with rare matching-number drivetrain components. It also has two build sheets. But these issues will need to be addressed in order to bring this Chevelle back to factory-correct status.\nMr. Nichols says that the Chevelle may change hands soon, but there's no info on how much the current owner wants for it. I can definitely say that it's a six-figure classic , but I wouldn't bet on it going for over $200,000. But it could become a quarter-million-dollar collectible with a bit of work. Check it out in the video below.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Chevelle 1970 Chevrolet Chevelle Chevrolet classic car muscle car\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Future Artemis Spacesuit Gets Sortie Twin, ISS Test Mission Being Planned",
